even expert raters call it a Tossup, and a Democratic-tilted national environment offsets Georgia's slight Republican lean"], "rationale": "Recent polls and prediction markets give Jackson a small edge, and his massive spending advantage shapes the closing stretch, but a favorable environment for Democrats and the state's near-even lean keep the race within about a point. With raters nearly unanimous at Tossup, this lands as a genuine tossup tilted slightly Republican.", "watch": "Any post-debate polling movement after the October 7 lone debate, or a late wave of outside spending, could shift a race this tight by several points.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Brian Kemp (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Georgia's governor's race is an open contest to succeed term-limited Republican Brian Kemp, pitting Democrat Keisha Lance Bottoms against Republican Rick Jackson. Polls since mid-September consistently show Jackson ahead by one to three points, and prediction markets give Bottoms roughly a 46 to 47 percent chance of winning. Still, six of seven expert raters call the race a Tossup, and the state's near-even partisan lean keeps either candidate within reach.\n\nMoney is the starkest difference: Jackson has raised about $119 million to Bottoms' $5.8 million, giving him a large advertising advantage down the stretch. A favorable national environment for Democrats and Bottoms' profile in metro Atlanta offset some of that. The two met in their only debate on October 7, clashing over the economy and Trump with few clear contrasts. A final margin of a point or two in either direction is the most likely outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.6414, "margin": 1.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5561, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5612, "margin": 0.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.5894, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5413, "margin": 0.8}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.5396, "margin"